The playbook, one fix per failure class:

```ts
// 1. ANY-TAKEOVER — quarantine at the BOUNDARY
catch (err: any) { /* every use of err is any */ }
// FIX:
catch (err: unknown) {
  if (err instanceof Error) showError(err.message);
  else showError(String(err));            // unknown FORCES narrowing
}
// API boundary — the honest pipeline:
const res: unknown = await response.json();   // NOT as User
if (isUser(res)) useUser(res);           // guard narrows to User

// 2. LOST GENERICS — the inference gave up
const [users, setUsers] = useState([]);  // inferred never[]!
users.push(u);                           // error: never
// FIX: pass the type argument:
const [users, setUsers] = useState<User[]>([]);

// 3. ASSERTION ABUSE — the uncheckable lie
const user = response as User;          // compiler BELIEVES you
user.profile.name;                       // runtime: undefined crash
// FIX — the guard the compiler CAN check:
function isUser(v: unknown): v is User {
  return typeof v === 'object' && v !== null && 'profile' in v;
}

// 4. NULL-CHECK WARS — absence made explicit
const name = user?.profile?.name ?? 'guest';  // optional chain + nullish
```

The any-contagion mechanism (why one any spreads): property access on any IS any — so any.b.c.d() taints every downstream binding; function PARAMS defaulted to any infect the return. That's why the fix is always at the BOUNDARY (catch, JSON, third-party untyped imports — declare a local .d.ts or module augmentation rather than @ts-ignore'ing call sites). The tsconfig escalation that prevents the class: noImplicitAny (untyped params ERROR — the flag that surfaces every implicit boundary) and strict as the family (strictNullChecks, strictFunctionTypes, strictBindCallApply...).

The as-ladder (what to reach for when the compiler complains, in order): (1) fix the real type — the mismatch is usually information (your shape is wrong, or a field is optional); (2) narrow with a guard/instanceof/in-checks; (3) satisfies (validate + preserve — the modern middle); (4) as — the LAST rung, legitimate for DOM refs (document.getElementById returns HTMLElement | null — you KNOW the layout), and library migrations. @ts-ignore/@ts-expect-error: expect-error is the self-testing form (it FAILS when the error disappears — stale suppressions surface), and both are TODO markers, not fixes.

The null-check migration reality (when strictNullChecks first lands): the error count spikes — that's the compiler FINDING latent bugs (the undefined-is-not-an-object class of production crashes). The triage order: make absence EXPLICIT (T | null), flow it honestly (return the union, don't hide it), consume with ?. and ??, and the discriminated-union upgrade when 'absent' and 'present' carry different payloads (loading/error/success).
